From f17a60f96efbea6ee43cf184b260e70554ad485e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vincent Leon <leon@mbp.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 09:34:41 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 08/11] Make push delivery pluggable
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Push delivery is hardcoded to Expo, so there is no way to add a second channel
without editing the send path. That blocks any platform Expo cannot reach —
HarmonyOS is the case in front of me, but the shape of the problem is not
specific to it.

This adds the seam and moves the existing Expo path behind it. Behaviour is
unchanged: same endpoint, same batch size, same ticket handling, same tokens on
disk. Adding a provider is now a directory under push/providers/ plus one line
in the manifest.

Two decisions worth reviewing:

Providers return dead tokens rather than revoking them. The store treats a
revocation as real only once it is durable — persist() throws on a write failure
and the caller keeps the previous state — and a provider holding a revoke
callback cannot honour that, so it would drop tokens that are still live on
disk. PushDeliveryResult.revokedTokens keeps that decision in the store.

Non-Expo tokens are stored under a separate `providers` key rather than in the
existing `subscriptions` array with a provider field. An older daemon reading
the same PASEO_HOME — a rollback, a downgrade, a parallel install — understands
`subscriptions` and ignores what it does not know, so it can never POST a
non-Expo token to Expo, and never revoke one off the back of an Expo ticket
error. A flat list with an added field loses that: the field is dropped on read
and erased on the next write.

The protocol carries an optional `provider` on register/unregister, and
server_info advertises `features.pushProviders` so a client only registers a
token for a channel this host can route. Absent provider means Expo, which is
the only thing it can have been.

One thing to note for future interface changes: the push mock in
websocket-server.relay-reconnect.test.ts is a vi.mock factory literal, which
TypeScript does not check against PushNotifications. Adding a method to the
interface broke it at runtime with no compile error. It is typed now.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
